WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.040
High impact starts with confidentiality, integrity, and availability.

00:00:04.040 --> 00:00:07.600
Confidentiality is about preventing unauthorized disclosure.

00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:11.615
Integrity is about preventing improper modification or loss of trust in information and

00:00:11.615 --> 00:00:12.530
processing.

00:00:12.530 --> 00:00:16.255
Availability is about making sure the service and data remain usable when needed.

00:00:16.255 --> 00:00:20.185
A high-impact context exists when a failure in one or more of these areas could cause

00:00:20.185 --> 00:00:22.260
severe or catastrophic adverse effects.

00:00:22.260 --> 00:00:26.355
High-impact cloud services may handle sensitive unclassified federal data,

00:00:26.355 --> 00:00:30.640
support mission-critical operations, affect public trust, influence privacy outcomes,

00:00:30.640 --> 00:00:34.950
or support activities where disruption can cascade into major operational or financial

00:00:34.950 --> 00:00:35.400
harm.

00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:36.515
In some contexts,

00:00:36.515 --> 00:00:40.340
protection-of-life considerations or essential public services may be part of the risk

00:00:40.340 --> 00:00:40.840
picture.

00:00:40.840 --> 00:00:43.540
The baseline should reflect the federal mission and data risk,

00:00:43.540 --> 00:00:45.640
not the provider's preferred sales position.

00:00:45.640 --> 00:00:46.990
Service dependency matters.

00:00:46.990 --> 00:00:51.415
A system that looks ordinary in isolation can become high impact if many agency workflows

00:00:51.415 --> 00:00:52.200
depend on it,

00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:54.000
if outages have wide consequences,

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:57.160
or if the service acts as a critical platform for other systems.

00:00:57.160 --> 00:01:01.570
Data sensitivity also matters, but availability and integrity can be just as important.

00:01:01.570 --> 00:01:05.730
A high-impact decision should consider the whole mission context rather than a single data

00:01:05.730 --> 00:01:06.100
label.

00:01:06.100 --> 00:01:10.340
The engineering implication is that High cannot be treated as a documentation exercise.

00:01:10.340 --> 00:01:14.700
Severe consequences require stronger attention to architecture, access control,

00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:18.711
monitoring, response, recovery, dependency management, and operational maturity.

00:01:18.711 --> 00:01:22.360
Teams should be able to explain not only what controls exist,

00:01:22.360 --> 00:01:25.060
but why those controls are suitable for the mission risk the service supports.
